t domains, which could lead to performance issues in larger networks. [Data: Entities (783), Relationships (1203)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Function of Routers in segmenting broadcast domains\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Routers operate at the Network Layer (Layer 3) of the OSI model and are critical for segmenting broadcast domains. Their primary purpose is to forward data packets between distinct networks or subnets using IP addresses. Routers act as boundaries, preventing broadcast traffic from propagating across different network segments. Each interface on a router typically belongs to a different broadcast domain. When a broadcast packet reaches a router interface, the router does not forward it to other interfaces, effectively containing the broadcast. This segmentation is crucial for network efficiency, better traffic management, and reduced bandwidth consumption. However, their advanced functionality can also introduce latency and increased complexity compared to simpler devices. [Data: Entities (784), Relationships (1223)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"VLANs for creating multiple smaller broadcast domains\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Virtual Local Area Networks (VLANs) provide a mechanism to create multiple smaller broadcast domains within a larger network. Unlike hubs or unconfigured switches that create a single broadcast domain, VLANs allow for the segmentation of network traffic at Layer 2. This segmentation is crucial for limiting the scope of broadcast traffic, which can otherwise consume excessive bandwidth and negatively impact network performance. By creating these smaller, more manageable domains, VLANs enhance network efficiency and security. [Data: Entities (280), Relationships (388)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Interplay between network devices and broadcast domain management\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The management of broadcast domains is heavily 